Name :
================
BPDU Filtering

Why we did this
================
BPDU Guard disables a port whenever a BPDU is received on an access port.

However, in some special environments, administrators want a port to:

- Operate as PortFast
- Ignore STP participation
- Not send BPDUs
- Not process received BPDUs

To achieve this, Cisco introduced BPDU Filtering.

BPDU Filtering suppresses BPDU transmission and reception on selected interfaces.

This effectively removes the port from STP operations.

What will happen
================
1. PortFast-enabled ports stop sending BPDUs.
2. Incoming BPDUs may be ignored.
3. The port behaves like a normal edge port.
4. STP does not build topology information through that port.
5. Faster port activation occurs.

Example

Without BPDU Filtering
----------------------

PC -------- Switch

Switch sends BPDUs periodically.

Result:
--------
Port participates in STP.


With BPDU Filtering
-------------------

PC -------- Switch

BPDUs Suppressed

Result:
--------
Port does not exchange BPDUs.

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Reduces unnecessary BPDU processing.
2. Useful for edge/user ports.
3. Faster network access for end devices.
4. Prevents STP interaction on selected ports.
5. Useful in controlled environments.
6. Works together with PortFast.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Can hide STP topology changes.
2. May create Layer 2 loops if misused.
3. Less secure than BPDU Guard.
4. Not recommended on switch-to-switch links.
5. Misconfiguration can cause serious network outages.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Administrators realized that end-user ports should come online immediately instead of waiting through STP states.

This led to:

PortFast

which allows access ports to transition immediately to the Forwarding state while remaining protected by BPDU Guard.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Enable BPDU Filtering Per Interface

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# spanning-tree bpdufilter enable


OR


Enable Globally on PortFast Ports

Switch(config)# spanning-tree portfast default
Switch(config)# spanning-tree portfast bpdufilter default

Verification
============

show spanning-tree interface fa0/1 detail

show spanning-tree summary

show running-config interface fa0/1

Expected Result
===============

BPDUs Sent:
-----------
Suppressed

BPDUs Received:
---------------
Ignored (depending on configuration)

STP Participation:
------------------
Disabled on that port

Port Status:
------------
Forwarding

Security:
---------
Reduced STP visibility, use with caution

Summary
=======

Problem:
Some edge ports do not require STP participation and administrators wanted to suppress BPDU processing.

Solution:
BPDU Filtering suppresses BPDU transmission and reception on selected ports.

Benefit:
Reduces STP interactions and allows simplified edge-port operation.

Limitation:
Can hide STP events and create loops if used incorrectly.

